-- check/good: a diamond of aliases is read in time linear in its depth
-- (checker-v2.md §7.4, amended 2026-11-06; #1475). `A{i} a = A{i-1} (A{i-1}
-- a)` gives the outer application a new argument at every level, and the
-- read keyed an alias by all its arguments' roots, so `A64 Int` expanded
-- 2^64 bodies and the check never ended. An alias is now keyed by the
-- arguments its type shows: the identity diamond forwards its argument,
-- `K` shows none, `P` shows `a` only. Chain.beni reads each in its own
-- module; this module reads them through Chain's interface (the
-- constructor, `unhide`, `name`, `boxed`) and through Chain's declarations
-- (the annotation of `six`). The golden is the interface, whose types keep
-- the names the program wrote. It is not a `run/` fixture: the gates check
-- every `run/` program with ownership v2 too, and v2 costs 4^depth on a
-- value of such a type (#2121).
import Chain exposing (Hidden)


pub hidden : Hidden
hidden = Hidden 5


pub six : Chain.A64 Int
six = Chain.unhide hidden + 1


pub label : String
label = Chain.name


pub fortyTwo : Int
fortyTwo = Chain.boxed.value + 1
